"Given that \\( f \\) is an invertible function from \\( (-\\infty, \\infty) \\) to \\( (-\\infty, \\infty) \\) and \\( f(f(x)) = x \\), we need to find \\( f(x) - f^{-1}(x) \\).\n\nFirst, let's consider the equation \\( f(f(x)) = x \\). Since \\( f \\) is invertible, we can apply \\( f^{-1} \\) to both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[ f^{-1}(f(f(x))) = f^{-1}(x) \\]\n\nUsing the property of inverse functions, \\( f^{-1}(f(y)) = y \\) and \\( f^{-1}(x) = x \\), we get:\n\n\\[ f^{-1}(x) = f(x) \\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\( f(x) - f^{-1}(x) \\). Using the result \\( f^{-1}(x) = f(x) \\), we have:\n\n\\[ f(x) - f^{-1}(x) = f(x) - f(x) = 0 \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\( \\boxed{0} \\) I hope it is correct."
"First, simplify the expression inside the logarithm:\n\n\\[\n64\\sqrt{8} = 64 \\cdot 2^{3/2} = 2^6 \\cdot 2^{3/2} = 2^{6 + 3/2} = 2^{15/2}\n\\]\n\nNext, simplify the base of the logarithm:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{8} = 2^{3/2}\n\\]\n\nNow, evaluate the logarithm:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{\\sqrt{8}}(64\\sqrt{8}) = \\log_{2^{3/2}}(2^{15/2})\n\\]\n\nUsing the change of base formula for logarithms, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{2^{3/2}}(2^{15/2}) = \\frac{\\log(2^{15/2})}{\\log(2^{3/2})} = \\frac{15/2}{3/2} = \\frac{15}{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\log_{\\sqrt{8}}(64\\sqrt{8})\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{15}{3}}.\\)"
"To simplify \\((3-i)(6+2i)\\), we use the distributive property (also known as the FOIL method for binomials):\n\n\\[\n(3-i)(6+2i) = 3 \\cdot 6 + 3 \\cdot 2i - i \\cdot 6 - i \\cdot 2i\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n= 18 + 6i - 6i - 2i^2\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\n= 18 + (6i - 6i) - 2i^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 18 + 0i - 2i^2\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(i^2 = -1\\):\n\n\\[\n= 18 - 2(-1)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 18 + 2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 20\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the simplified form of \\((3-i)(6+2i)\\) is \\(\\boxed{20}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $20$. I hope it is correct."
