"To find what power of 4 is equal to 8, we need to solve the equation \\(4^x = 8\\).\n\nFirst, we recognize that 8 can be expressed as a power of 2:\n\\[ 8 = 2^3 \\]\n\nSo, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[ 4^x = 2^3 \\]\n\nNext, we express 4 as a power of 2:\n\\[ 4 = 2^2 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (2^2)^x = 2^3 \\]\n\nUsing the property of exponents \\((a^m)^n = a^{mn}\\), we get:\n\\[ 2^{2x} = 2^3 \\]\n\nSince the bases are the same, we can equate the exponents:\n\\[ 2x = 3 \\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nTherefore, the power of 4 that is equal to 8 is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\\)."
"We start with the system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2x - 3y = 8 \\\\\n4x + 3y = -2\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we can simplify the second equation by dividing all terms by 3:\n\\[\n4x + 3y = -2 \\implies 2x + y = -2\n\\]\n\nNow, we have:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2x - 3y = 8 \\\\\n2x + y = -2\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNext, we add the two equations together to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\\[\n(2x - 3y) + (2x + y) = 8 + (-2)\n\\]\n\\[\n4x = 6\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 1\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(x = 1\\) back into the second equation:\n\\[\n2(1) + y = -2\n\\]\n\\[\n2 + y = -2\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -4\n\\]\n\nThe product of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is \\(\\boxed{-4}\\)."
