"To solve the equation \\( |2 - |x|| = 1 \\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The equation \\( |2 - |x|| = 1 \\) can be split into two separate equations:\n\n1. \\( 2 - |x| = 1 \\)\n2. \\( 2 - |x| = -1 \\)\n\nLet's solve each equation separately:\n\n1. \\( 2 - |x| = 1 \\)\n   \\[\n   |x| = 1\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\( x = \\pm 1 \\).\n\n2. \\( 2 - |x| = -1 \\)\n   \\[\n   |x| = 3\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\( x = \\pm 3 \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of the squares of all real values of \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n(1)^2 + (-1)^2 + (3)^2 + (-3)^2 = 1 + 1 + 9 + 9 = 20\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the squares is \\( \\boxed{20}\\)."
