"To find the value of \\((a) \\times (b)\\) when \\(a = 3\\) and \\(b = 5\\), we start with the given expression:\n\n\\[\n(a) \\times (b) = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(a = 3\\) and \\(b = 5\\) into the expression:\n\n\\[\n(3) \\times (5) = 3^2 + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 + 5^2\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term separately:\n\n\\[\n3^2 = 9\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 = 30\n\\]\n\\[\n5^2 = 25\n\\]\n\nAdd these values together:\n\n\\[\n9 + 30 + 25 = 64\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\((a) \\times (b)\\) when \\(a = 3\\) and \\(b = 5\\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{64}\\). I hope it is correct."
"To simplify $\\frac{1+\\sqrt{2}}{2+\\sqrt{3}}$, we need to rationalize the denominator. We multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, which is $2-\\sqrt{3}$.\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1+\\sqrt{2}}{2+\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{2-\\sqrt{3}}{2-\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{(1+\\sqrt{2})(2-\\sqrt{3})}{(2+\\sqrt{3})(2-\\sqrt{3})}\n\\]\n\nThe denominator simplifies as follows:\n\n\\[\n(2+\\sqrt{3})(2-\\sqrt{3}) = 2^2 - (\\sqrt{3})^2 = 4 - 3 = 1\n\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(1+\\sqrt{2})(2-\\sqrt{3})}{1} = 2 - \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, $A+B+C+D = 2 + 2 + 6 + 2 = \\boxed{12}$.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $12$. I hope it is correct."
