"Let $l$ be the number of lemon candies, $p$ be the number of peppermint candies, $c$ be the number of chocolate candies, and $v$ be the number of vanilla candies. We have the following equations:\n\n1. $l + p + c + v = 15$\n2. $p + l = 2(c + v)$\n3. $p = l + 8$\n\nSubstitute $p = l + 8$ into the first equation:\n\n$$l + (l + 8) + c + v = 15$$\n$$2l + 8 + c + v = 15$$\n$$2l + c + v = 7$$\n\nNow, substitute $p = l + 8$ into the second equation:\n\n$$(l + 8) + l = 2(c + v)$$\n$$2l + 8 = 2(c + v)$$\n\nWe already have $2l + c + v = 7$, so:\n\n$$7 = l + 8$$\n$$l = 1$$\n\nTherefore, the number of lemon candies is $\\boxed{1}$."
"First, simplify the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{24}} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3456}}{9} + \\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nSimplify each term:\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{24}} = \\dfrac{4}{4} = 1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3456}}{9} = \\dfrac{54}{9} = 6\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{6} = \\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nCombine these results:\n\n\\[\n1 + 6 + \\sqrt{6} = 7 + \\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nNow, divide by \\(\\sqrt{6}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{7 + \\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} = \\dfrac{7}{\\sqrt{6}} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} = 7 + 1 = 8\n\\]\n\nFinally, take the square root:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{8} = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified expression is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."
"To solve the equation \\(\\frac{3x^2-4x+1}{x-1}=m\\), we first simplify the left-hand side. We can use polynomial division to simplify \\(\\frac{3x^2-4x+1}{x-1}\\).\n\nPerforming the division:\n\n\\[\n3x^2 - 4x + 1 \\div (x-1) = 3x - 1 + \\frac{2}{x-1}\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n3x - 1 + \\frac{2}{x-1} = m\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x \\neq 1\\), the term \\(\\frac{2}{x-1}\\) is always positive and can take any positive value. Therefore, \\(m\\) can take any positive value.\n\nTo find the values that \\(m\\) cannot have, we note that \\(m\\) cannot be negative because the term \\(\\frac{2}{x-1}\\) is always positive. Therefore, the only value that \\(m\\) cannot have is zero.\n\nThus, the real value that \\(m\\) can NOT have is \\(\\boxed{0}\\)."
