"We are given two equations based on the values of the algebraic expression \\(a + bx\\):\n\n1. When \\(x = 2\\), \\(a + 2b = 15\\).\n2. When \\(x = 5\\), \\(a + 5b = 3\\).\n\nWe can solve these equations simultaneously to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nFirst, subtract the first equation from the second equation:\n\\[\n(a + 5b) - (a + 2b) = 3 - 15\n\\]\n\\[\n3b = -12\n\\]\n\\[\nb = -4\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(b = -4\\) back into the first equation:\n\\[\na + 2(-4) = 15\n\\]\n\\[\na - 8 = 15\n\\]\n\\[\na = 19\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(a + b\\):\n\\[\na + b = 19 + (-4) = 15\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum \\(a + b\\) is \\(\\boxed{15}\\)."
