"To solve the equation \\( x = \\frac{1}{2 + \\frac{1}{x-2}} \\), we start by clearing the fraction in the denominator. Multiply both sides by \\( x-2 \\):\n\n\\[ x(x-2) = \\frac{1}{2 + \\frac{1}{x-2}} \\cdot (x-2) \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[ x(x-2) = \\frac{x-2}{2} \\]\n\nNext, multiply both sides by 2 to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[ 2x(x-2) = x - 2 \\]\n\nExpand and simplify:\n\n\\[ 2x^2 - 4x = x - 2 \\]\n\nRearrange the equation to set it to zero:\n\n\\[ 2x^2 - 5x + 2 = 0 \\]\n\nThis can be factored as:\n\n\\[ (x-2)^2 = 0 \\]\n\nThus, the only solution is:\n\n\\[ x = 2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the positive solution to the equation is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{2} \\]"
"To find the length of the segment of the number line consisting of the points that satisfy \\((x-4)^2 \\le 9\\), we start by solving the inequality:\n\n\\[\n(x-4)^2 \\le 9\n\\]\n\nFirst, we take the square root of both sides of the inequality. Remember that taking the square root of both sides gives us two separate inequalities:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{9} \\le x-4 \\le \\sqrt{9}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n3 \\le x-4 \\le 3\n\\]\n\nNext, we add 4 to all parts of the inequality to solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n1 \\le x \\le 7\n\\]\n\nThe segment of the number line consisting of the points that satisfy \\((x-4)^2 \\le 9\\) is from \\(x = 1\\) to \\(x = 7\\). To find the length of this segment, we subtract the smaller endpoint from the larger endpoint:\n\n\\[\n7 - 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the segment of the number line is \\boxed{6}."
