"To determine the probability that both Dan and Freddie win the class president and vice president positions, we need to consider the probability of each candidate winning their respective position and then multiply these probabilities together.\n\n1. **Probability that Dan wins class president:**\n   - Dan has a 50% chance of winning, which is \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\n2. **Probability that Freddie wins vice president:**\n   - Freddie also has a 50% chance of winning, which is \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\nSince the events of Dan winning class president and Freddie winning vice president are independent, we multiply their probabilities together:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{Dan wins class president and Freddie wins vice president}) = P(\\text{Dan wins class president}) \\times P(\\text{Freddie wins vice president})\n\\]\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{Dan wins class president and Freddie wins vice president}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that both Dan and Freddie win is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]"
