"To determine the number of ways to put 5 distinguishable balls into 3 distinguishable boxes, we need to consider the choices available for each ball.\n\n1. Each ball has 3 choices: it can go into any of the 3 boxes.\n2. Since the balls are distinguishable, the choices for each ball are independent of the others.\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to distribute the 5 balls into the 3 boxes is given by the product of the number of choices for each ball:\n\n\\[\n3 \\text{ (choices for the first ball)} \\times 3 \\text{ (choices for the second ball)} \\times 3 \\text{ (choices for the third ball)} \\times 3 \\text{ (choices for the fourth ball)} \\times 3 \\text{ (choices for the fifth ball)}\n\\]\n\nCalculating this, we get:\n\n\\[\n3 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 = 3^5 = 243\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of ways to put 5 distinguishable balls into 3 distinguishable boxes is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{243}\n\\]"
