"Given the right triangle \\(JKL\\) with \\(\\angle JLK = 90^\\circ\\), we know that \\(KL = 8\\) and \\(\\tan K = \\frac{15}{8}\\). We need to find \\(JL\\).\n\nIn a right triangle, the tangent of an angle is defined as the ratio of the opposite side to the adjacent side. Therefore, \\(\\tan K = \\frac{JL}{KL}\\).\n\nGiven \\(\\tan K = \\frac{15}{8}\\) and \\(KL = 8\\), we can set up the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{JL}{8} = \\frac{15}{8}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(JL\\), we can cross-multiply:\n\\[\nJL \\cdot 8 = 15 \\cdot 8\n\\]\n\\[\nJL \\cdot 8 = 120\n\\]\n\nNow, divide both sides by 8:\n\\[\nJL = \\frac{120}{8}\n\\]\n\\[\nJL = 15\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\(JL\\) is 15.\n\nSolution: \\(\\boxed{15}\\)"
