"The volume \\( V \\) of a cone is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h \\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the radius of the base and \\( h \\) is the height of the cone.\n\nGiven that the volume \\( V \\) is \\( 30 \\pi \\) cubic inches and the radius \\( r \\) is 3 inches, we can substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[ 30 \\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (3)^2 h \\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\n\\[ 30 \\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 9 \\cdot h \\]\n\n\\[ 30 \\pi = 3 \\pi h \\]\n\nTo solve for \\( h \\), divide both sides of the equation by \\( 3 \\pi \\):\n\n\\[ h = \\frac{30 \\pi}{3 \\pi} \\]\n\n\\[ h = 10 \\]\n\nTherefore, the cup needs to be \\(\\boxed{10}\\) inches tall to hold \\( 30 \\pi \\) cubic inches of water."
"To find the sum of the measures of the interior angles in a convex hexagon, we can use the formula for the sum of the interior angles of a polygon. The formula is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of interior angles} = (n - 2) \\times 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the number of sides of the polygon. For a hexagon, \\( n = 6 \\).\n\nSubstituting \\( n = 6 \\) into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of interior angles} = (6 - 2) \\times 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n6 - 2 = 4\n\\]\n\nNow, multiply by \\( 180^\\circ \\):\n\n\\[\n4 \\times 180^\\circ = 720^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the measures of the interior angles in a convex hexagon is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{720^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(720^\\circ\\). I hope it is correct."
