"Let the base area of the larger prism be \\( A \\) and the height of the larger prism be \\( h \\).\n\nThe volume of the larger prism is:\n\\[ V_{\\text{large}} = A \\cdot h \\]\n\nThe base area of the smaller prism is \\( \\frac{A}{4} \\) and the height of the smaller prism is \\( \\frac{h}{2} \\).\n\nThe volume of the smaller prism is:\n\\[ V_{\\text{small}} = \\left( \\frac{A}{4} \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\frac{h}{2} \\right) = \\frac{A \\cdot h}{8} \\]\n\nThe ratio of the volume of the smaller prism to the volume of the larger prism is:\n\\[ \\frac{V_{\\text{small}}}{V_{\\text{large}}} = \\frac{\\frac{A \\cdot h}{8}}{A \\cdot h} = \\frac{1}{8} \\]\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the volume of the smaller prism to the volume of the larger prism is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}\\)."
"To find the length of the median \\( BM \\) in the right triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\) with legs \\( AB = 4\\sqrt{2} \\) and \\( BC = 2 \\), we first need to determine the length of the hypotenuse \\( AC \\).\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nAC^2 = AB^2 + BC^2\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = (4\\sqrt{2})^2 + 2^2\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = 16 \\cdot 2 + 4\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = 32 + 4\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = 36\n\\]\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\nThe median \\( BM \\) in a right triangle to the hypotenuse is half the length of the hypotenuse. Therefore:\n\\[\nBM = \\frac{AC}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the median \\( BM \\) is \\( \\boxed{3} \\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $3$. I hope it is correct."
