"We can use the AM-GM inequality to find the largest possible value of the given expression.\n\nThe AM-GM inequality states that for any non-negative real numbers $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$,\n\n$$\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n}{n} \\ge \\sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n}.$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\sqrt{a} + \\sqrt{b} + \\sqrt{c}}{3} \\ge \\sqrt[3]{\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b} \\cdot \\sqrt{c}} = \\sqrt[3]{abc},$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\sqrt{a + b + c} \\ge \\sqrt[3]{abc}.$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\frac{\\sqrt{a} + \\sqrt{b} + \\sqrt{c}}{\\sqrt{a + b + c}} \\le \\frac{3\\sqrt[3]{abc}}{\\sqrt[3]{abc}} = 3.$$\n\nThus, the largest possible value of the given expression is\n\n$$\\boxed{3}.$$\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $3$. I hope it is correct."
