"Let's denote the two prime numbers as \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) such that \\( p > q \\). According to the problem, the difference between the two prime numbers is 17:\n\n\\[ p - q = 17 \\]\n\nWe need to find the sum of these two prime numbers, \\( p + q \\).\n\nFirst, let's consider the smallest prime number, which is 2. If \\( q = 2 \\), then:\n\n\\[ p - 2 = 17 \\]\n\\[ p = 19 \\]\n\nNow, we need to check if 19 is a prime number. A prime number is a number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself. 19 is indeed a prime number because it is only divisible by 1 and 19.\n\nSo, the two prime numbers are \\( p = 19 \\) and \\( q = 2 \\).\n\nThe sum of these two prime numbers is:\n\n\\[ p + q = 19 + 2 = 21 \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the two prime numbers is \\(\\boxed{21}\\)."
