"To determine the next time the two cyclists will cross the starting line together, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of their lap times. The first cyclist takes 12 minutes per lap, and the second cyclist takes 16 minutes per lap.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of the lap times:\n\\[ 12 = 2^2 \\times 3 \\]\n\\[ 16 = 2^4 \\]\n\nThe LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:\n\\[ \\text{LCM} = 2^4 \\times 3 = 16 \\times 3 = 48 \\]\n\nSo, the two cyclists will cross the starting line together every 48 minutes.\n\nNext, we convert 48 minutes into hours and minutes:\n\\[ 48 \\text{ minutes} = 0 \\text{ hours and } 48 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nTherefore, the next time they cross the starting line together is at 0:48 PM.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{0:48}\\)."
