"To solve the expression \\(\\left(\\dfrac{7}{16}\\right)^{111} \\times \\left(\\dfrac{16}{7}\\right)^{111}\\), we can use the properties of exponents. Specifically, we can use the property that states \\(a^m \\times a^n = a^{m+n}\\).\n\nLet's rewrite the expression using this property:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{16}\\right)^{111} \\times \\left(\\dfrac{16}{7}\\right)^{111} = \\left(\\dfrac{7}{16} \\times \\dfrac{16}{7}\\right)^{111}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the fraction inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{7}{16} \\times \\dfrac{16}{7} = 1\n\\]\n\nSo, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n1^{111}\n\\]\n\nSince any number raised to the power of 111 is 1, we have:\n\n\\[\n1^{111} = 1\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{1}\\)."
