"First, let's find the area of the original circle. The formula for the area \\( A \\) of a circle is:\n\n\\[ A = \\pi r^2 \\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the radius. Given that the radius \\( r \\) is 2 inches, the area of the original circle is:\n\n\\[ A_{\\text{original}} = \\pi (2)^2 = 4\\pi \\text{ square inches} \\]\n\nNext, let's find the area of the new circle when the radius is doubled. The new radius is \\( 2 \\times 2 = 4 \\) inches. The area of the new circle is:\n\n\\[ A_{\\text{new}} = \\pi (4)^2 = 16\\pi \\text{ square inches} \\]\n\nThe increase in area is the difference between the new area and the original area:\n\n\\[ \\text{Increase in area} = A_{\\text{new}} - A_{\\text{original}} = 16\\pi - 4\\pi = 12\\pi \\text{ square inches} \\]\n\nTherefore, the area is increased by \\( \\boxed{12 \\pi} \\) square inches."
