"To solve the expression \\(\\frac{7+21}{14+42}\\), we first perform the addition in the numerator and the denominator.\n\nIn the numerator:\n\\[ 7 + 21 = 28 \\]\n\nIn the denominator:\n\\[ 14 + 42 = 56 \\]\n\nSo, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[ \\frac{28}{56} \\]\n\nNext, we simplify the fraction \\(\\frac{28}{56}\\) by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 28 and 56. The GCD of 28 and 56 is 4.\n\nWe divide both the numerator and the denominator by their GCD:\n\\[ \\frac{28 \\div 4}{56 \\div 4} = \\frac{7}{14} \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression \\(\\frac{7+21}{14+42}\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{7}{14}} \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $\\frac{7}{14}$. I hope it is correct."
