"Let the measure of the smallest angle be \\( x \\) degrees. Since the largest angle is twice the measure of the smallest angle, the largest angle is \\( 2x \\) degrees. The other two angles are right angles, so they are both 90 degrees each.\n\nThe sum of the interior angles in a quadrilateral is 360 degrees. Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\n\\[ x + 2x + 90 + 90 = 360 \\]\n\nSimplifying this equation:\n\n\\[ 3x + 180 = 360 \\]\n\nSubtract 180 from both sides:\n\n\\[ 3x = 180 \\]\n\nDivide both sides by 3:\n\n\\[ x = 60 \\]\n\nSo, the measure of the smallest angle is 60 degrees. The largest angle is twice this measure:\n\n\\[ 2 \\times 60 = 120 \\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of the largest angle is \\boxed{120} degrees.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $120$. I hope it is correct."
"To compute \\( \\frac{1}{31} \\div \\frac{1}{93} \\), we can use the property of fractions that states dividing by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying by its reciprocal. Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{31} \\div \\frac{1}{93} = \\frac{1}{31} \\times \\frac{93}{1} \\]\n\nNext, we perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{31} \\times \\frac{93}{1} = \\frac{1 \\times 93}{31 \\times 1} = \\frac{93}{31} \\]\n\nNow, we simplify the fraction \\( \\frac{93}{31} \\). The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 93 and 31 is 31. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by their GCD:\n\n\\[ \\frac{93 \\div 31}{31 \\div 31} = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]"
