"To determine how many trips around Earth you can take if you travel one billion meters, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Convert the circumference of Earth from kilometers to meters.\n2. Divide the total distance traveled by the circumference of Earth in meters.\n\nFirst, let's convert the circumference of Earth from kilometers to meters:\n\\[ 40,000 \\text{ kilometers} = 40,000 \\times 1000 \\text{ meters} = 40,000,000 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\nNext, we divide the total distance traveled by the circumference of Earth in meters:\n\\[ \\frac{1,000,000,000 \\text{ meters}}{40,000,000 \\text{ meters}} = \\frac{1,000,000,000}{40,000,000} = 25 \\]\n\nTherefore, you can take 25 trips around Earth if you travel one billion meters.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is 25 trips. I hope it is correct."
"To find the area of the gray figure, we need to calculate the areas of the three squares and then subtract the areas of the overlapping regions.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of one square:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one square} = 6^2 = 36 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nSince there are three squares, the total area of the three squares is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 36 = 108 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the area of the overlapping regions. Notice that the squares overlap such that each square is covered by two other squares. Therefore, the area of the overlapping regions is equal to the area of one square:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of overlapping regions} = 36 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nNow, we subtract the area of the overlapping regions from the total area of the three squares to find the area of the gray figure:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of gray figure} = 108 - 36 = 72 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer:\n\\[\n\\boxed{72}\n\\]"
"To determine how many positive integers are less than \\(200\\pi\\), we first need to approximate the value of \\(200\\pi\\).\n\nUsing the approximation \\(\\pi \\approx 3.14159\\), we calculate:\n\\[\n200\\pi \\approx 200 \\times 3.14159 = 628.318\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the largest integer less than 628.318. The largest integer less than 628.318 is 628.\n\nNow, we count the positive integers from 1 to 628. This is a sequence of integers starting from 1 and ending at 628. The number of terms in this sequence is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Number of terms} = \\text{last term} - \\text{first term} + 1 = 628 - 1 + 1 = 628\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of positive integers less than \\(200\\pi\\) is 628.\n\nFinal Answer: \\boxed{628}"
