"To find the interior degree measure of angle \\( A \\), we need to use the fact that the sum of the interior angles in a pentagon is \\( 5 \\times 180^\\circ = 900^\\circ \\).\n\nGiven the angles of the pentagon:\n- One angle is \\( 100^\\circ \\).\n- Another angle is \\( 110^\\circ \\).\n- A third angle is \\( 40^\\circ \\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of these three angles:\n\\[\n100^\\circ + 110^\\circ + 40^\\circ = 250^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract this sum from the total sum of the interior angles in a pentagon to find the measure of angle \\( A \\):\n\\[\n900^\\circ - 250^\\circ = 650^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the interior degree measure of angle \\( A \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{650^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\( A = 650^\\circ \\). I hope it is correct."
