"To solve the equation \\(5x - 3 = 12\\), we first need to isolate \\(x\\).\n\n1. Add 3 to both sides of the equation:\n   \\[\n   5x - 3 + 3 = 12 + 3\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   5x = 15\n   \\]\n\n2. Divide both sides by 5:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5x}{5} = \\frac{15}{5}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   x = 3\n   \\]\n\nNow that we have \\(x = 3\\), we need to find the value of \\(5x + 3\\).\n\n3. Substitute \\(x = 3\\) into the expression \\(5x + 3\\):\n   \\[\n   5(3) + 3 = 15 + 3 = 18\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(5x + 3\\) is \\(18\\). I hope it is correct."
