"To find the area outside the smaller region but inside the larger region, we need to calculate the area of the larger circle and subtract the area of the smaller circle.\n\nThe formula for the area of a circle is \\( A = \\pi r^2 \\), where \\( r \\) is the radius.\n\nFirst, calculate the area of the larger circle:\n\\[ A_{\\text{large}} = \\pi (10)^2 = 100\\pi \\]\n\nNext, calculate the area of the smaller circle:\n\\[ A_{\\text{small}} = \\pi (1)^2 = \\pi \\]\n\nNow, subtract the area of the smaller circle from the area of the larger circle to find the area outside the smaller region but inside the larger region:\n\\[ A_{\\text{region}} = A_{\\text{large}} - A_{\\text{small}} = 100\\pi - \\pi = 99\\pi \\]\n\nTherefore, the area outside the smaller region but inside the larger region is \\(\\boxed{99\\pi}\\) square inches.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(99\\pi\\). I hope it is correct."
